WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.320
Let us understand disaster recovery.

2
00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:06.140
A student keeps a second copy
of schoolwork in another safe

3
00:00:06.140 --> 00:00:09.460
place and practises opening
it on a different computer.

4
00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:13.760
Knowing a copy exists is not
enough if it is unreadable, its

5
00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:16.580
password is lost or it takes days to find.

6
00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:20.640
Recovery is the complete
ability to resume useful work.

7
00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:25.000
Disaster recovery is a tested
plan for restoring required

8
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:27.860
service and data after
a serious disruption.

9
00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:32.340
Define the acceptable recovery
time and acceptable data-loss

10
00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:35.860
window, then prove your
procedure can meet them.

11
00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:39.960
Follow the normal path. Define
how long the required user

12
00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:43.580
operation may be unavailable
and how much recent data loss

13
00:00:43.580 --> 00:00:47.900
is acceptable. These are
explicit business objectives, not

14
00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:51.940
product defaults. Capture
suitable backups and change logs

15
00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:54.240
with recoverable keys and configuration.

16
00:00:55.060 --> 00:00:58.900
Live replication alone may
copy deletion or corruption to

17
00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:03.060
every current replica. SeatSpot
needs a response to regional

18
00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:06.040
loss, accidental deletion or corruption.

19
00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:10.920
Application replicas alone do
not restore deleted orders or

20
00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:15.660
missing secrets/configuration.
Now change one condition.

21
00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:19.280
A serious failure interrupts
the production environment.

22
00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:23.280
Recovery needs an environment,
permissions and an executable

23
00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:26.540
runbook, not merely a
promise that a backup exists.

24
00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:30.760
A common misunderstanding is
this: Replication replaces

25
00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:35.120
recovery backups. Replication
may reproduce corruption or

26
00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:38.380
deletion. Independent recovery
points solve a different

27
00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:42.140
problem. Think about this.
Hourly-only backups are

28
00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:45.900
available. Do they satisfy
a 15-minute recovery-point

29
00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:49.340
objective by themselves?
Pause the video and make your

30
00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:53.120
prediction. No. The missing-data
window can approach an

31
00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:57.680
hour. Use a suitable capture/recovery
strategy and prove it

32
00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:01.400
in a drill. Let us reason
with a concrete example.

33
00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:06.820
The business requires an
illustrative RTO of 60 minutes and

34
00:02:06.820 --> 00:02:11.660
RPO of 15 minutes. Backups run every hour.

35
00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:15.020
Hourly-only backups can leave
nearly an hour of missing

36
00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:21.840
data, exceeding a 15-minute
RPO. A 60-minute RTO also

37
00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:25.060
includes provisioning,
restore, validation and traffic

38
00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:27.980
recovery, not just file download time.

39
00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:32.500
Select a suitable backup/log
capture strategy and rehearse

40
00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:36.300
the whole path. Targets are
objectives; measured drill

41
00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:39.460
results establish whether
the current plan meets them.

42
00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:42.460
These figures are teaching
examples, not production

43
00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:46.300
guarantees. Turn the idea
into a design decision.

44
00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:50.340
Rebuild required infrastructure
and restore data in a safe

45
00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:54.920
environment. Include secrets,
certificates, queues and other

46
00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:57.760
dependencies required for
actual user workflows.

47
00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:01.220
Query restored data and
run correctness checks.

48
00:03:01.220 --> 00:03:04.720
A process reporting restore
complete does not prove that

49
00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:08.220
bookings, ownership and payment
reconciliation are usable.

50
00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:11.840
When you try the practice
task, look for this result.

51
00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:16.060
An existing backup is not proof
of a usable recovery plan;

52
00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:18.460
measured restoration is the evidence.

53
00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:23.060
Check your understanding. Restore
traffic safely and compare

54
00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:25.900
measured end-to-end recovery
and data loss with the

55
00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:30.000
objectives. Record gaps, improve
the runbook and repeat the

56
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:34.220
drill. Here is a challenge. A
restore finishes in 40 minutes

57
00:03:34.460 --> 00:03:38.120
but application validation
and routing take another 35.

58
00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:43.260
Does it meet a 60-minute
RTO? Pause before the answer.

59
00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:47.940
No: the measured end-to-end
recovery is 75 minutes.

60
00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:51.700
Improve or revise the plan
based on business requirements

61
00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:55.320
and repeat the drill. For an
interview, choose a recovery

62
00:03:55.320 --> 00:03:57.560
strategy and measure the complete drill.

63
00:03:58.020 --> 00:04:00.620
Say what you would measure,
not only which tool you would

64
00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:00.900
buy.
